when i resize the compose preview panel the preview windows inside get resized too, shouldnt happen unless there is too little space for them.
   -> DONE. Tiles were stored as fractions of the canvas, so resizing rescaled them. Now pixel-stable.

the produced eggs iso was 5.9gb it should be closer to 3.9gb something big does not get excluded. is puppeteer still in?
   -> DONE (measured the actual shipped tree, not guessed). Puppeteer was NOT it - it is already gone from the project; its 1.3G cache lives in .cache which was already excluded.
   -> The real leak: ~/caspar-build 3.3GB (the CasparCG build tree) + 407M apt cache + 79M crash dumps + 183M boost headers. Now excluded -> should land ~4.1GB.
   -> The apt cache leaked because of a real bug: the exclude had TWO patterns on ONE line, and mksquashfs reads one per line, so it matched nothing. Test added so that cannot recur.
   -> STILL ~1.6GB available: var/lib/snapd holds dead snap revisions + bases for the REMOVED firefox snap. Fix is on the build host BEFORE produce (snap remove --purge + refresh.retain=2), not an exclude - excluding leaves broken mount units at boot. Would take the ISO to ~3.0GB.
   -> SEPARATE PROBLEM FOUND: no Chrome binary ships on the ISO (chromium/chrome not installed, .cache excluded), so template thumbnail rendering will fail on a booted ISO. Pre-existing, needs a decision.

write a new md file in work folder giving me a breakdown of options for porting the webui into a native ubuntu gui app. electron is a no go for me.
name the file NATIVE_GUI_PROPOSITIONS
   -> DONE: work/NATIVE_GUI_PROPOSITIONS.md. Honest answer: DON'T port. Every native option is 40-80 person-weeks = 9-15 month release slip for zero new operator features.
   -> Why: your client/server split is already clean (163 API paths + 21 WS messages is the whole contract), which is the prize people port to get. Also the ISO argument fails - Firefox cannot be removed because your browser SOURCES are Firefox. And performance cannot improve: video never enters the browser.
   -> The one real argument for native: a native window could shape ITSELF and delete ~600-900 lines of the hole-punching hack. Worth ~1 engineer-month, not 40-80.
   -> Cheap follow-up found: previs (4,324 lines) may be dead code - three.js loads from /vendor/three/ which does not exist. Worth confirming.

